issue_context.body:
### Expected behavior

ByteBuf.capacity() should be able to successfully resize up to maxCapacity regardless of it's initial state.  

### Actual behavior

If two zero length buffers are created and resized to 17, an AssertionError is thrown:

```
java.lang.AssertionError
	at __randomizedtesting.SeedInfo.seed([7DAACA0E7D79473:4594B38D5FFB07BE]:0)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolChunk.initBufWithSubpage(PoolChunk.java:573)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolThreadCache$SubPageMemoryRegionCache.initBuf(PoolThreadCache.java:315)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolThreadCache$MemoryRegionCache.allocate(PoolThreadCache.java:376)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolThreadCache.allocate(PoolThreadCache.java:167)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolThreadCache.allocateSmall(PoolThreadCache.java:151)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolArena.tcacheAllocateSmall(PoolArena.java:148)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolArena.allocate(PoolArena.java:134)
	at io.netty.buffer.PoolArena.reallocate(PoolArena.java:286)
	at io.netty.buffer.PooledByteBuf.capacity(PooledByteBuf.java:118)
```

Note: Initializing the two buffers with a length of 1 appears to fix the issue.

### Steps to reproduce

### Minimal yet complete reproducer code (or URL to code)

```
    @Test
    public void testNetty() {

        ByteBufAllocator allocator = new PooledByteBufAllocator(true);

        ByteBuf a = allocator.buffer(0, 16384);
        ByteBuf b = allocator.buffer(0, 16384);

        System.out.println("a.capacity(" + 16 + ")");
        a.capacity(16);
        System.out.println("b.capacity(" + 16 + ")");
        b.capacity(16);
        System.out.println("a.capacity(" + 17 + ")");
        a.capacity(17);
        System.out.println("b.capacity(" + 17 + ")");
        b.capacity(17);

    }
```

### Netty version

4.1.70.FINAL

### JVM version (e.g. `java -version`)

17.0.1

