After a bit more digging, I realized that this issue only surfaces when io.netty.allocator.directMemoryCacheAlignment is set to a positive value.  Here's a test that fails:

```
public class NettyTest {

    static {
        System.setProperty("io.netty.allocator.directMemoryCacheAlignment", "64");
    }

    @Test
    public void testNetty() {

        ByteBufAllocator allocator = new PooledByteBufAllocator(true);

        ByteBuf a = allocator.buffer(0, 16384);
        ByteBuf b = allocator.buffer(0, 16384);

        System.out.println("a.capacity(" + 16 + ")");
        a.capacity(16);
        System.out.println("b.capacity(" + 16 + ")");
        b.capacity(16);
        System.out.println("a.capacity(" + 17 + ")");
        a.capacity(17);
        System.out.println("b.capacity(" + 17 + ")");
        b.capacity(17);

    }

}
```
